\section*{The separable quotient assertion for a real-valued-measurable continuum}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 323; 2026-09-23), the asserted result is that the general separable-quotient assertion is independent of ZFC relative to a measurable cardinal, with a ZFC-only relative-independence result at density $\aleph_1$.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For $\mathbb F=\mathbb R$ or $\mathbb C$, write $\mathrm{SQ}_{\mathbb F}$ for the following assertion: each Banach space of infinite dimension over $\mathbb F$ admits a bounded linear quotient whose range is separable and remains infinite dimensional. In ZFC, the manuscript claims
\[
 \mathfrak c\text{ real-valued measurable}\Rightarrow\mathrm{SQ}_{\mathbb F},
 \qquad\mathrm{CH}\Rightarrow\neg\mathrm{SQ}_{\mathbb F}.
\]
Here real-valued measurable means a probability measure on every subset of the cardinal $\mathfrak c=2^{\aleph_0}$, vanishing on singletons and additive on disjoint families of size less than $\mathfrak c$. If ZFC plus a measurable cardinal is consistent, the separable quotient assertion is therefore claimed independent of ZFC. Relative consistency of its failure needs only consistency of ZFC.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 323; 2026-09-23), a coded coordinate predual uses norming paths to prevent separable evaluation images in the counterexample model. Block restrictions and strictly singular estimates establish the obstruction, while a continuum measure hypothesis supplies the positive-model direction.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 323; 2026-09-23).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 323; 2026-09-23), the documented Lean scope is under CH, real and complex Banach counterexamples with no infinite-dimensional separable quotient; the positive-model direction and complete relative-independence assertion are outside the selected theorem.

This coverage does not establish a formal proof of the complete problem as stated here.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{SeparableQuotientNegative.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.SeparableQuotient.negative\_main}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

